module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.recursive_frame_demands_one_child_twice

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data recursive_frame_demands_one_child_twice: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "recursive_frame_demands_one_child_twice" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "recursive frame demands one child twice (INVALID STATE: in a recursive fold, one frame asks for the same child's result more than once along one reachable path. Either a speculative rendering is bound eagerly, read by only some arms, and recomputed by the arm that discards it, or a call is made once to test `!= none` and again to use the value. When that child position can hold the same construct, the waste nests and cost is 2^depth in the source's nesting, while the output is unchanged.)",
    "SPECIMENS (v1.compiler.emit_rust, fixed together): emit_typed_method_call rendered the receiver and first argument before the template lookup and re-rendered both in emit_rust_generic_method_call when no template row existed; its plain arm called rust_callable_field_method_call twice. emit_rust_map_method_call rendered the receiver eagerly and re-rendered it as the owned iterator, and rendered the lambda eagerly and re-rendered its body. emit_rust_higher_order_method did the same to its lambda. emit_typed_match rendered every arm and then re-rendered every arm in string-from mode. emit_typed_field_access rendered the base and re-rendered it for a one-field anonymous record. v1.compiler.emit emit_algebra_method_call_unified re-rendered the first argument on its bridge arm, the same defect in the Go and Python path. The real-path trigger was std.types commit_sha_text_holds, a 16-deep nested `replace` added 2026-09-24, which every fixture importing std.types re-emits, so fixture emission came to dominate the required floor's claim fold.",
    "INSTRUMENT: `gunbc compile --source-dir <dir> --output-dir <out> --target rust` over a one-file module whose body nests `replace(...)` in receiver position to depth d, or a `|> map(...)` chain of length k; the run prints the compile.emit duration. Before the fix the duration quadruples per two levels of nesting; after it the duration is flat, and the emitted files are byte-identical across the two binaries.",
    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the emitted text is byte-identical before and after, so no assertion on output can go red; the required floor subtracts a fixture compile's cost as shared fill, so no per-claim clock can go red either. It is not identity_absent_graph_traversal (the frame already holds the value, so no key is missing and a memo would be the wrong repair, DESIGN section 2: it would make authored duplication cheap). It is a candidate contributor to emit_stage_cost_dominates_its_closure, not measured on that closure.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: outside the ladder, a cost defect with no executing budget on any route. The existing output assertions and the floor's per-claim clock do not detect it; a structural producer re-entry witness is missing, and that witness COULD fail. CEILING: structurally guaranteed, a proof from modeled structure that a recursive emitter frame evaluates each rendering judgment at most once on every path. NEXT TRIGGER, as a capability: a count of repeated evaluation of the SAME rendering judgment along one path -- the child together with the scope, mode, target and other inputs the rendering reads, so that a second visit under a different judgment (string-from mode, a lambda scope) is not a violation -- derived from the fold's structure (v1.compiler.complexity) and enrolled over the seed emitters, with a discriminating red on a planted duplicate call whose output is identical. Until then this row is review diligence only, and filing it does not make recurrence mechanically prevented. NOT FIXED HERE, recorded for the same review: v2.compiler.translate translate_algebra folds an Arrow's children twice, and v1.compiler.trait_derive_emit v1_type_expr_mentions_param_non_phantom walks a container's children twice, where collapsing the walk is not obviously answer-preserving.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
